#: ../../book/index.rst:1
msgid ""
"Impact of Export Controls on the Future of Open Source AI: how software, "
"model weights, and silicon hardware gates reshape human progress, examined "
"with primary sources and an illustrative game."
msgstr "以原始来源和示意博弈分析出口管制如何影响软件、模型、硬件及开放AI的未来。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:5
msgid "Impact of Export Controls on the Future of Open Source AI"
msgstr "出口管制对开源人工智能未来的影响"

#: ../../book/index.rst:8
msgid ""
"An idea can be shared without using it up. Thomas Jefferson expressed this "
"in an 1813 letter: *\"He who lights his taper at mine, receives light "
"without darkening me.\"* That image captures the promise of open-source AI: "
"one team can learn from another without taking its knowledge away. "
"`JEFFERSON "
"<https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Jefferson/03-06-02-0322>`_"
msgstr ""
"知识可以分享而不被耗尽。杰斐逊1813年以蜡烛比喻： *借我的火点亮蜡烛，获得光而不使我变暗。*  一个团队可以向另一个学习而不夺走其知识。 "
"`JEFFERSON "
"<https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Jefferson/03-06-02-0322>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:10 ../../book/index.rst:15
msgid ""
"A border barrier holds up computer parts and technical plans while two "
"research teams try to work together."
msgstr "两支研究团队试图合作，边境障碍却阻滞了计算机部件和技术图纸的流动。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:17
msgid ""
"Open-weight models can give researchers and smaller organizations more "
"control over how they use AI. A clinic, a language project or a university "
"might adapt a public model on its own computers. These are illustrative "
"uses, not documented deployments or proof that a model is clinically safe. "
"Downloadable weights also differ from the fuller requirements of the Open "
"Source AI Definition. `OSI <https://opensource.org/ai/open-source-ai-"
"definition>`_ `R1 <https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1>`_ `QWEN "
"<https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen3>`_"
msgstr ""
"开放权重可能使小机构更有自主权。诊所、语言项目或大学可能在本地改进模型。这些是说明性用途，不是有记录的应用或临床安全证明；可下载权重也不同于完整开放AI定义。"
" `OSI <https://opensource.org/ai/open-source-ai-definition>`_ `R1 "
"<https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1>`_ `QWEN "
"<https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen3>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:19
msgid ""
"Then the walls began to rise: not only in the sky of software, but deep "
"underground in the bedrock of silicon."
msgstr "随后，高墙开始拔地而起：不仅横亘在软件的苍穹之上，更深扎进地底硅基的岩床之中。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:21
msgid "Not Just Software or Weights: The Hardware Beneath Open Minds"
msgstr "不止于软件与权重：托起开放心智的硬件底座"

#: ../../book/index.rst:24
msgid ""
"Most conversations about open-source AI make a comforting mistake. They "
"imagine an AI model as pure, weightless poetry: a file of code and numbers "
"that can stay free forever as long as someone places an MIT or Apache 2.0 "
"license on GitHub."
msgstr ""
"绝大多数关于开源人工智能的讨论，都怀着一种令人宽慰的错觉。人们习惯把人工智能模型想象成纯粹而失重的诗篇：仿佛只要有人在 GitHub 上挂一份 MIT "
"或 Apache 2.0 许可证，那一串代码与数字便能永远自由。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:26
msgid ""
"Open models depend on physical computers. Large training runs can need "
"advanced processors, memory and fast networks; smaller models can run on "
"less demanding equipment. Not every open-model user needs the latest "
"manufacturing process or a cluster of ten thousand cards. Controls on scarce"
" hardware can nevertheless affect which teams can train, inspect and deploy "
"models."
msgstr ""
"模型依赖实体计算机。大型训练可能需要先进处理器、存储和网络，小模型可用较普通设备。不是所有使用者都需最新制程或万卡集群，但稀缺硬件仍可能影响谁能训练、检查和部署。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:28 ../../book/index.rst:33
msgid ""
"Open models depend on chips, memory and computing infrastructure. A "
"restriction on one part can affect access to the whole system."
msgstr "开放模型依赖芯片、存储与算力设施。限制一部分可能影响整体可用性。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:35
msgid ""
"**Hardware and design knowledge:** US controls cover specified chips, "
"manufacturing items and some foreign-made items under defined conditions. "
"China proposes controls on specified processor and memory designs, and on "
"certain technologies for clusters with **more than 10,000** accelerator "
"cards. Design knowledge, finished chips and clusters are different objects "
"of control. `US26 "
"<https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-01-15/html/2026-00789.htm>`_ "
"`EAR734 <https://www.bis.gov/regulations/ear/734>`_ `EAR742 "
"<https://www.bis.gov/regulations/ear/742>`_ `CN-A1 "
"<https://insanai.github.io/aiexportcontrols/sources/announcement-48-attachment-1.pdf>`_"
msgstr ""
" **硬件与设计知识：**  美国覆盖指定物项及符合条件的部分外国物项。中国提议覆盖指定设计和  **超过1万张**  "
"加速卡集群的部分技术。设计知识、成品芯片和集群是不同管制对象。 `US26 "
"<https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-01-15/html/2026-00789.htm>`_ "
"`EAR734 <https://www.bis.gov/regulations/ear/734>`_ `EAR742 "
"<https://www.bis.gov/regulations/ear/742>`_ `CN-A1 "
"<https://insanai.github.io/aiexportcontrols/sources/announcement-48-attachment-1.pdf>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:36
msgid ""
"**Models and data:** Announcement 48 began temporary registration for "
"covered transfers on **16 October 2026**. Its proposed catalogue includes "
"model core technology meeting any of three tests: training calculation, "
"parameter count or a qualifying global top-10 assessment. These proposed "
"categories are separate from the registration already operating and from the"
" US model-weight rule. `CN48 "
"<https://www.mofcom.gov.cn/zwgk/zcfb/art/2026/art_0a31a3c9ac404f7a9b0e917ce3412997.html>`_"
" `CN-A1 "
"<https://insanai.github.io/aiexportcontrols/sources/announcement-48-attachment-1.pdf>`_"
" `US25 "
"<https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-01-15/html/2025-00636.htm>`_ "
"`GAO26 <https://www.gao.gov/products/b-337935>`_"
msgstr ""
" **模型与数据：**  中国于  **2026年10月16日**  "
"启动临时登记。目录拟以计算量、参数或合格前十评估作为替代条件。拟议类别、当前登记与美国权重规则须分开。 `CN48 "
"<https://www.mofcom.gov.cn/zwgk/zcfb/art/2026/art_0a31a3c9ac404f7a9b0e917ce3412997.html>`_"
" `CN-A1 "
"<https://insanai.github.io/aiexportcontrols/sources/announcement-48-attachment-1.pdf>`_"
" `US25 "
"<https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-01-15/html/2025-00636.htm>`_ "
"`GAO26 <https://www.gao.gov/products/b-337935>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:40
msgid ""
"Even if software licenses remain free on paper, open-source AI cannot thrive"
" in a vacuum: if export controls ration the chips needed to train open "
"models, lock up open silicon blueprints, or price local hardware out of "
"reach for hospitals and universities, humanity gets nudged back from owning "
"minds to renting metered corporate cloud windows."
msgstr ""
"哪怕软件许可证在纸面上依旧自由，开源人工智能也绝无可能在真空中繁茂生长：一旦出口管制配给甚至卡住训练开源模型所需的算力芯片、锁死开放芯片架构蓝图，或是让医院与大学再也买不起本地部署硬件，全人类就将在不知不觉间被推回老路：从真正拥有心智工具，退化为按量租用科技巨头的云端计费窗口。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:42
msgid "The Fortress Trap: How Both Sides Can Lose"
msgstr "堡垒陷阱：双方如何一起受损"

#: ../../book/index.rst:45
msgid ""
"The central argument of this book is that broad restrictions on shared "
"knowledge can leave both countries worse off. We can show one way this "
"happens with a simple game. We cannot prove that every export control has "
"this effect merely by choosing numbers for a table."
msgstr "本书的核心论点是广泛限制共享知识可能使双方更差。简单博弈展示一种机制，但选择一组数字不能证明所有管制都有同样效果。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:47
msgid ""
"Imagine two countries choosing between **Share (S)** and **Wall (W)**. "
"Sharing means allowing ordinary research and open-model cooperation; a wall "
"means broadly restricting it. This deliberately simplifies real policy. "
"Targeted controls, legitimate security benefits, third countries and unequal"
" access to chips are not represented in the first example."
msgstr ""
"两国选择  **共享（S）**  或  **筑墙（W）** "
"。共享代表普通研究合作，筑墙代表广泛限制。这简化了政策，最初没有计入精准管制、安全收益、第三国或不平等算力。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:51
msgid ""
"**Assumptions, not measurements.** The scores below are invented teaching "
"values. They express preferences within this example, not dollars, measured "
"innovation or national welfare. The table does not estimate a 70% loss or a "
"fourfold increase in danger."
msgstr " **假设，不是测量。**  分数是虚构教学值，代表偏好而非金钱、实测创新或国家福利。表格没有估计70%损失或四倍危险。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:53 ../../book/index.rst:58
msgid ""
"Two teams finish a computer by sharing parts and knowledge, then struggle "
"when a wall separates them. This illustrates the assumed game, not a "
"measured outcome."
msgstr "两队共享部件和知识时能完成计算机，被墙隔开后却难以完成。这是书中假设博弈的示意，不是实测结果。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:62
msgid "Assumed scores in the teaching game"
msgstr "教学博弈中的假设得分"

#: ../../book/index.rst:62
msgid ""
"#import \"figures/diagrams.typ\": game-theory-matrix\n"
"#game-theory-matrix(lang: \"en\")"
msgstr ""
"#import \"figures/diagrams.typ\": game-theory-matrix\n"
"#game-theory-matrix(lang: \"zh_CN\")"

#: ../../book/index.rst:69
msgid "Reading the Four Outcomes"
msgstr "读懂四种结果"

#: ../../book/index.rst:72
msgid ""
"**Both share:** A gets 17 and B gets 17. In this example, both value the "
"benefits of exchange."
msgstr " **双方共享：**  A得17、B得17。例中假定双方重视交流。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:73
msgid ""
"**A shares, B restricts:** A gets 3 and B gets 20. We assume B gains a "
"temporary advantage while still using A's public work."
msgstr " **A共享、B限制：**  A得3、B得20。假设B仍使用A公开工作并获得短暂优势。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:74
msgid ""
"**A restricts, B shares:** A gets 20 and B gets 3. This is the mirror image."
msgstr " **A限制、B共享：**  A得20、B得3，是镜像情形。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:75
msgid ""
"**Both restrict:** A gets 5 and B gets 5. We assume lost cooperation and "
"duplicated effort outweigh the security benefits of these broad walls."
msgstr " **双方限制：**  各得5。假设合作损失与重复工作超过这些广泛壁垒的安全收益。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:77
msgid ""
"If B shares, A prefers 20 to 17. If B restricts, A prefers 5 to 3. B faces "
"the same choices. A wall is therefore each country's best response whatever "
"the other chooses. Under these assumptions, mutual restriction is a **Nash "
"equilibrium**: neither can improve its score by changing its choice alone. "
"Yet both would prefer 17 to 5. This is a **Prisoner's Dilemma**. Mutual "
"sharing is a **Pareto-efficient** outcome here, but that term does not mean "
"every country gets its largest possible individual score. `GAME "
"<https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~rafael/networks-html/chapter1.html>`_"
msgstr ""
"B共享时，A更喜欢20而非17；B限制时，A更喜欢5而非3。B亦然。因此在这些假设下，相互限制是  **纳什均衡** "
"，任何一方单独改变都不能提高得分。双方却都更想要17而非5，这就是  **囚徒困境** 。共享在此是帕累托有效，不意味着每方取得最高个人分数。 "
"`GAME <https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~rafael/networks-html/chapter1.html>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:79
msgid ""
"The conclusion depends on the ordering **20 > 17 > 5 > 3**, not on those "
"particular numbers. It identifies a possible strategic trap. It does not "
"measure the actual costs of US or Chinese policy, and it does not establish "
"that an outcome outside this small table is impossible."
msgstr "结论依赖  **20 > 17 > 5 > 3**  的排序而非具体数字。它指出可能的战略陷阱，不测量现实成本，也不排除表外结果。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:81
msgid "What Could Change the Result?"
msgstr "什么可能改变结果？"

#: ../../book/index.rst:84
msgid ""
"A control that prevents a serious misuse could provide security benefits "
"large enough to change the ranking. A chip bottleneck, different national "
"capabilities or an effective agreement could also change incentives. Export "
"controls do not automatically prevent domestic publication, and public "
"access does not automatically make a system safe. Those questions need "
"evidence beyond this example. `NTIA "
"<https://www.ntia.gov/sites/default/files/publications/ntia-ai-open-model-"
"report.pdf>`_ `OPENRISK <https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07918>`_"
msgstr ""
"避免严重滥用的管制可能带来足够安全收益，改变排序。芯片瓶颈、能力差异或有效协议也会改变激励。出口管制不自动阻止国内公开，公开也不自动安全；这些问题需额外证据。"
" `NTIA <https://www.ntia.gov/sites/default/files/publications/ntia-ai-open-"
"model-report.pdf>`_ `OPENRISK <https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07918>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:86
msgid ""
"Countries also interact repeatedly. In this simplified game, suppose "
"cooperation continues while both share, but a deviation earns 20 once and is"
" followed by 5 forever. Let :math:`\\delta` represent how much a country "
"values the next round relative to this one. Cooperation is at least as "
"attractive as that deviation when:"
msgstr ""
"国家会反复互动。例中双方共享就继续合作，一次偏离可得20，此后永远得5。:math:`\\delta` "
"表示下一轮相对当下的价值。合作至少同样有利的条件是："

#: ../../book/index.rst:88
msgid ""
"\\frac{17}{1-\\delta} \\ge 20 + \\frac{5\\delta}{1-\\delta}\n"
"\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\delta \\ge \\frac{1}{5}"
msgstr ""
"\\frac{17}{1-\\delta} \\ge 20 + \\frac{5\\delta}{1-\\delta}\n"
"\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\delta \\ge \\frac{1}{5}"

#: ../../book/index.rst:93
msgid ""
"This is a calculation inside the assumed game, not a diplomatic forecast. It"
" assumes an indefinite relationship, observable choices and a credible "
"response to deviation. Errors, hidden actions or a known final round can "
"undermine that strategy. The useful policy question is how to make continued"
" cooperation credible, rather than treating mistrust as inevitable. `GAME "
"<https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~rafael/networks-html/chapter1.html>`_"
msgstr ""
"这是假设博弈内的计算，不是外交预测。它假定无限期关系、可观察选择和可信回应；错误、隐藏行动或已知最后一轮可能破坏策略。关键在如何使持续合作可信。 "
"`GAME <https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~rafael/networks-html/chapter1.html>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:95
msgid "Why More Review Can Help, Without Guaranteeing Safety"
msgstr "更多审查可能有益，但不保证安全"

#: ../../book/index.rst:98
msgid ""
"Open access can make independent testing and repair easier. A simple "
"probability example explains one possible benefit. If a flaw exists with "
"probability :math:`p_0` and each of :math:`n` teams independently detects it"
" with probability :math:`q`, then:"
msgstr ""
"开放可方便独立测试与修复。若缺陷存在概率为 :math:`p_0`，每个独立团队发现概率为 :math:`q`，共有 :math:`n` 个团队，则："

#: ../../book/index.rst:100
msgid "P(\\text{flaw remains undetected}) = p_0(1-q)^n"
msgstr "P(\\text{flaw remains undetected}) = p_0(1-q)^n"

#: ../../book/index.rst:104
msgid ""
"For a flaw known to exist and an assumed detection chance of one half, one "
"team misses it with probability 50%, four independent teams with 6.25%, and "
"ten with about 0.098%. These are conditional calculations. Real teams can "
"share blind spots, lack testing resources or find a flaw without fixing it. "
"An undetected-flaw probability is not a measure of total cyber, biological "
"or societal risk."
msgstr ""
"已知存在缺陷且每队发现概率假定一半时，1队漏检50%，4队6.25%，10队约0.098%。这是条件计算。现实团队可能盲点相同、资源不足，或发现后未修复。漏检概率不是总体网络、生物或社会风险。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:106
msgid ""
"Open weights can also help harmful actors modify a system or remove "
"safeguards. NTIA's 2024 report considered both benefits and risks, "
"recommending monitoring rather than restricting then-current open weights. "
"Research on open foundation models stresses the need to compare the extra "
"risk with existing alternatives. Neither source establishes a universal "
"safety multiplier for openness. `NTIA "
"<https://www.ntia.gov/sites/default/files/publications/ntia-ai-open-model-"
"report.pdf>`_ `OPENRISK <https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07918>`_"
msgstr ""
"公开权重也可能方便恶意修改或删除防护。NTIA在2024年权衡收益与风险，建议监测当时的开放权重。研究强调比较相对现有替代品的新增风险；两者均未确立普遍的安全倍数。"
" `NTIA <https://www.ntia.gov/sites/default/files/publications/ntia-ai-open-"
"model-report.pdf>`_ `OPENRISK <https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07918>`_"

#: ../../book/index.rst:108
msgid ""
"Our recommendation is to make controls precise, assess their security "
"benefit against their cost to cooperation, and protect workable routes for "
"legitimate open research. That is an argument informed by the evidence, not "
"a theorem that every restriction harms everyone."
msgstr "我们主张精准管制，比较安全收益与合作成本，并保护合法开放研究的可行路径。这是基于证据的观点，不是所有限制伤害所有人的定理。"

#: ../../book/index.rst:110
msgid "The Central Factual Discovery: An Ajar Doorway, Not a Sealed Wall"
msgstr "本书的核心实证发现：一扇微启的通道，而非封死的高墙"

#: ../../book/index.rst:113
msgid ""
"When we turn from game theory back to the verified legal texts of November "
"2026, we find something remarkable: despite alarming headlines, regulators "
"in both Beijing and Washington have deliberately left real bridges standing "
"for open-source AI:"
msgstr ""
"当我们从数理博弈论回到截至 2026 年 11 "
"月经逐字核验的官方文本时，一个值得深思的事实浮现出来：尽管舆论场上的标题惊心动魄，北京与华盛顿的立法者却都不约而同地为开源人工智能特意留出了真实的通行桥梁："

#: ../../book/index.rst:115 ../../book/index.rst:120
msgid ""
"Computing, biomedicine and solar manufacturing, shown through a processor, a"
" cell and the layers of a solar panel."
msgstr "处理器、细胞和太阳能板的分层结构，分别展示计算、生物医药和光伏制造。"

